# Settings for the Berth deployment bundle. Copy this file to .env, then run `docker compose up -d`.
# Nothing here is secret: API keys and service passwords are set in Berth's setup wizard and stored
# in its database under /config.
#
# One Berth per host. The project name, container names (berth, berth-*), network name and subnet
# (172.28.0.0/16) are fixed in the compose file, so a second copy on the same host (say, one for real
# use and one for trying things out) collides on names and the subnet and will not start. Changing
# the ports below is not enough.

# The media root. The berth, qbittorrent and jellyfin containers all mount this one folder (prowlarr
# never touches files); the download folder and the libraries both live under it.
# Hardlinks only work within one filesystem, so mount this single root and do not mount downloads and
# libraries separately (that gives EXDEV, and Berth's Route check refuses it). It cannot be exFAT or a
# network share.
# The default ./data, next to the compose file, is fine for a trial. For real use, point it at your
# media disk:
#   Linux:   DATA_ROOT=/srv/berth/data
#   Windows: DATA_ROOT=C:\Berth\data
#   Unraid:  DATA_ROOT=/mnt/user/<share>/Berth
DATA_ROOT=./data

# The parent of the four services' config folders: berth, qbittorrent, jellyfin and prowlarr appear
# under it. Backing up Berth means backing up ${CONFIG_ROOT}/berth.
#   Linux:   CONFIG_ROOT=/srv/berth/config
#   Windows: CONFIG_ROOT=C:\Berth\config
CONFIG_ROOT=./config

# The user the containers run as. Set it to the host account that owns DATA_ROOT, so both ends of a
# hardlink have the same owner. On Linux, `id -u` and `id -g`; on Unraid, 99 / 100. Bind mounts on
# Windows have no owner, so leave the defaults there.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000

# Permission mask for new files. 022 means files 644 and folders 755 (the TRaSH Guides value).
UMASK=022

# The containers' time zone, used for log times and schedules (the nightly reconcile). Change it to
# yours, e.g. Europe/London or America/New_York.
TZ=Asia/Taipei

# The five published ports. Change one only when it clashes with something else on this host; then
# `docker compose up -d` recreates the containers. Do not edit the compose file for this.
# Berth cannot see the host side from inside its container, so JELLYFIN_PORT, QBITTORRENT_WEBUI_PORT
# and PROWLARR_PORT are passed to it too: the first is the port "Open in Jellyfin" links use, the
# second is how it reaches the bundled qBittorrent, and the third is the port the wizard's "add a
# private indexer in Prowlarr" link opens.
BERTH_PORT=8383
JELLYFIN_PORT=8096
# Both qBittorrent ports use the same number inside and outside the container. The web UI's Host
# check compares the port as well, so remapping only the outside (18080 to 8080 inside) makes the
# browser get 401; the BitTorrent port is the one announced to other peers, so it must be the one
# that actually accepts connections.
QBITTORRENT_WEBUI_PORT=8080
QBITTORRENT_BT_PORT=6881
PROWLARR_PORT=9696

# The bundled services to start; berth itself has no profile and always starts.
# For each service you choose "Existing" for in the wizard, remove it here:
#   Only your own Jellyfin:          COMPOSE_PROFILES=qbittorrent,prowlarr
#   All three are your own:          COMPOSE_PROFILES=
# Removing one only stops later `docker compose up -d` runs from starting it; it does not stop one
# that is already running (Compose leaves services outside the active profiles alone). If it already
# started, also run `docker compose stop jellyfin` (with the ones you removed).
# Forgetting is not fatal: the bundled one keeps running (its container is named berth-*, so it does
# not clash with your own jellyfin) and only wastes resources. But if it uses the same port as yours,
# it is the one that fails to start (change its *_PORT above, or remove it).
COMPOSE_PROFILES=jellyfin,qbittorrent,prowlarr
